&lt;h2&gt;Profound vs. Athena AI for AEO at a Glance&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Profound&lt;/strong&gt; is built around an “AI Marketer” — a credit-based system of agents, a context manager that stores your brand knowledge, and a monitoring layer (Answer Engine Insights, Prompt Volumes) that feeds it.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Its current pricing structure is unusually simple: a free Trial and a custom-quoted Enterprise plan. There is no published self-serve paid tier for individual brands at the time of writing, a meaningful change from the $99–$399/month plans that Profound previously publicized and that many older reviews still cite.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Athena AI&lt;/strong&gt;, sold under the AthenaHQ brand, is built around a credit-based Starter plan with a real published price ($295/month), plus a free Essential tier and a custom Enterprise tier. It leans harder on “action” features out of the box — content optimization agents, direct Shopify and GA4 integrations, and an agency-specific program with prospecting tools.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The core trade-off between the two platforms: Profound’s only self-serve option today is a time-boxed free trial, with everything else routed through a sales conversation; Athena AI publishes a real self-serve price for a useful feature set, but gates its most distinctive capability (the Athena Citation Engine) and multi-region tracking behind Enterprise.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Methodology note:&lt;/strong&gt; Figures above come from Profound’s pricing page (tryprofound.com/pricing), Athena AI’s plans page (athenahq.ai/plans), and each vendor’s SafeBase-hosted trust center, all pulled and cross-checked on September 23, 2026.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;G2 review counts and star ratings move constantly — treat them as directional, not exact, and check the live listing before you cite a number in a business case.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;Which covers more AI engines?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;On paper, Athena AI’s self-serve Starter plan lists 11 named models—including ChatGPT, Perplexity, Google AI Overviews, Google AI Mode, Gemini, Claude, Copilot, Grok, DeepSeek, Meta AI, and Mistral—with additional models available on request.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Profound’s equivalent self-serve access (the free Trial) covers only three: ChatGPT, Gemini, and Google AI Overviews. Profound’s full published ceiling — up to 9 engines, including Claude, Copilot, DeepSeek, Google AI Mode, and Exa Search — is available only on its custom-quoted Enterprise plan.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;That is a real, verifiable difference in what you can access without a sales call. It is not, on its own, a reason to pick either tool.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Watch out: A longer list of supported AI engines is only useful if those engines are the ones your actual buyers use.&lt;/strong&gt; A B2B software brand whose prospects live in ChatGPT and Perplexity gets little marginal value from Grok or Mistral coverage.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Before comparing engine counts, pull whatever session or referral data you already have (Google Analytics, server logs, or your CRM’s source field) to see which AI referrers are already sending you traffic, and prioritize coverage of those first.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Regional and language reach follows the same pattern on both platforms: broad, named coverage sits behind Enterprise. Profound’s Trial is limited to one language and one region; its Enterprise plan lists “custom” languages and regions with no published number.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Athena AI’s Starter plan is explicitly single-region and single-language; multi-region and multi-language tracking is an Enterprise-only line item. If you’re evaluating either tool for a global brand, don’t assume the self-serve tier will get you there — budget for the sales conversation that unlocks true international coverage.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Our guide on &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/which-llm-should-you-use"&gt;which LLM you should use&lt;/a&gt; is a useful companion here, since engine prioritization and model selection are really the same decision viewed from two different desks.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pro tip:&lt;/strong&gt; Ask each vendor for a same-week snapshot of your brand across their full engine list, even if it require...

&lt;p&gt;An AEO checker tells you whether the AI answers your buyers rely on actually mention your brand. People increasingly ask ChatGPT, Perplexity, and Gemini a question and act on the reply without clicking a link, so visibility that once showed up in your rankings can vanish into an answer you never see.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=9dd5e54b-fbef-4dd0-bc44-1689feb1ea18&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Get Started with HubSpot's AEO Tool" height="58" width="335" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/9dd5e54b-fbef-4dd0-bc44-1689feb1ea18.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To manage that, you need to see which questions surface your content, which reward a competitor, and where an engine gets your brand wrong. A good AEO checker measures exactly that, and the tools do it in very different ways.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This guide covers what an AEO checker does, how to run manual checks across ChatGPT, Perplexity, and AI Overviews, and how the best AEO checkers compare so you can pick the right fit. Read on to discover the software that’ll help you adapt to this &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/adapting-to-the-new-era-of-search"&gt;new era of search&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
