&lt;h2&gt;What is answer engine optimization?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/answer-engine-optimization"&gt;Answer engine optimization (AEO)&lt;/a&gt; is the practice of improving how often and how accurately your brand appears in the AI-generated answers people get from tools like ChatGPT, Perplexity, and Gemini. Read more about this shift in search behavior in our &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/generative-engine-optimization"&gt;guide to generative engine optimization&lt;/a&gt;. Those tools are answer engines; rather than returning a page of links, they read across many sources and reply with one synthesized answer, often resolving a question before anyone clicks through. Google brought the same summary-first behavior to its own results when it &lt;a href="https://blog.google/products-and-platforms/products/search/ai-overviews-update-may-2024/"&gt;launched AI Overviews in May 2024&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AEO and SEO address different halves of the same problem. SEO works to rank your pages in a list of links; AEO works to get your brand mentioned and your content cited inside the answer itself. The two are complementary rather than competing, and strong SEO still supplies the crawlability and authority signals that answer engines rely on when they choose what to cite.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;That citation goes to the content answer engines can parse and trust: clearly structured pages, direct answers to real questions, and claims backed by verifiable primary sources.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;What does an AEO checker do?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;An AEO checker measures how visible your brand is across answer engines, then turns that visibility into recommendations on how you can improve. It checks:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Whether ChatGPT, Perplexity, and Gemini cite your webpages or mention your brand&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Whether you appear in Google AI Overviews&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Entity and &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/structured-data-testing-tool"&gt;schema readiness&lt;/a&gt; so that answer engines can interpret your content&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;How extractable your answers are for reuse in AI summaries&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;From there, a checker ranks what to fix first, flagging the content updates, schema corrections, and entity improvements most likely to move citations.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.hubspot.com/hubfs/aeo-checker-2-20260903-852936.webp" style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block; width: 450px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;" alt="AEO checker workflow diagram showing five steps: measure, diagnose, prioritize, optimize, and report in a continuous loop"&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;How to Run an AEO Check in Google AI Overviews&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Checking your visibility in AI Overviews is a manual process to start: you run your priority queries in a live search session and read what Google returns. Google’s AI Overviews (AIOs) surface only &lt;a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/ai-features%23:~:text%3DAI%2520Overviews%2520are%2520only%2520shown%2520when%2520our%2520systems%2520determine%2520that%2520it%2520is%2520additive%2520to%2520classic%2520Search%252C%2520and%2520as%2520such%252C%2520often%2520don%2527t%2520trigger."&gt;when Google’s systems decide a summary adds something&lt;/a&gt; beyond the standard links, so they don’t trigger for every query. An AIO can also vary from one session to the next, so sampling across a few sessions beats a single lookup. When one appears, it cites the pages it drew from. As you can see in the screenshot below, Google places source links inline, next to the text they support, with a desktop hover preview naming the site.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.hubspot.com/hubfs/aeo-checker-3-20260903-8904553.webp" style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block; width: 650px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;" alt="Google AI Overview results for 'how to qualify leads in sales' showing inline citations and related content snippets highlighted"&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Step 1: Define your important queries.&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;List the queries that matter: your brand name, branded product terms, and the informational questions your buyers ask. Run each in a signed-out or incognito session, and record three things: whether an AI Overview appears, whether your domain shows up among its inline source links, and the exact claim it makes.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Step 2: Interpret results.&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Compare the queries that trigger an AIO and cite you against those where an AIO appears but competitors are cited instead of you. That second group is your content gap list. Because AIOs vary between sessions, treat a single absence as a prompt to re-check, not proof you’ve lost a placement.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Step 3: Build reporting.&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Screenshot each result with the query, date, and location for a defensible before-and-after record. Don’t count on Search Console to isolate this for you. Its standard Performance report rolls...

